// A call site can state the instantiation itself: `Repo::<Todo>.new("todos")`.
//
// Until now the instantiation reached a generic constructor only from an **expected type** — an
// annotated binding, a declared return, a field's declared type, or a parameter's. All four still
// work and are pinned in `call_site_type_argument_positions.noe`. What none of them could do is
// make the call SELF-SUFFICIENT: with no expected type anywhere, `r = Repo.new("todos")` had no
// instantiation to record, and there was no way to write one down.
//
// The type arguments travel the channel a VALUE receiver's already do (`recv_args`), so nothing
// downstream is special-cased: the class's parameters are seeded exactly as for `r.method(…)`, and
// the same construction-site recording tags the object. The oracle is the tag itself — `model()`
// reads `type_name::<T>()` off the object at run time, and the two repositories must disagree.
//
// `::<>` and not `<>`: a bare `<` after an identifier is ambiguous with less-than in expression
// position, so `Repo<Todo>.new(…)` stays a parse error (`call_site_type_argument_bare_angle.noe`).
// expect: stdout "todos:Todo"
// expect: stdout "users:User"
// expect: stdout "Type.Class(Repo, [Type.Struct(Todo, [])])"
// expect: stdout "false"
// expect: exit 0
struct Todo {
    id: int
}
struct User {
    id: int
}
class Repo<T> {
    pub tbl: string

    pub fn new(tbl: string): Repo<T> {
        return Repo { tbl: tbl };
    }

    pub fn model(): string {
        return self.tbl ~ ":" ~ type_name::<T>();
    }
}
rt = Repo::<Todo>.new("todos");
ru = Repo::<User>.new("users");
echo rt.model();
echo ru.model();
dt: dyn = rt;
du: dyn = ru;
echo type_of(dt);
echo type_of(dt) == type_of(du);
